From 524a1bbf32293412a321f911a773c1e771fce060 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 14:28:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] repack: CPU kernels for the 128-group ternary format

The block template derived its group size from the bit width, so it could not
express 2-bit at group 128 and a g128 model fell back to per-row vec_dot. Take
the group size as a defaulted third parameter and template the AVX512-VNNI
GEMV/GEMM on it; every existing block<K,N> spelling stays valid.

The vector core needed nothing: the expansion helper loads one 32-byte register
and has no notion of the block, and the qs stride is a constant of the four-row
interleave rather than of the group.

g64 and g128 get separate arms and separate instantiations. A g128 row in a g64
kernel is mis-strided silent wrong math, not a crash, so the type travels as a
template parameter into the repack, which asserts it. The old early-return
tripwire becomes a real arm and keeps its reason in-comment.

Origin: lane-110F, mission #1 (g128 CPU repack kernels)
Measured-effect: correctness only; speed UNMEASURED. RIG-A (Beelink SER7,
  Ryzen 7840HS + Radeon 780M), llama-server, Ternary-Bonsai-8B-Q2_0.g128.gguf,
  -ngl 0 --no-host -c 2048 -t 8, temp 0, token-id comparison, 3 rolls per cell:
  repacked output token-identical to the scalar path on both a GEMV- and a
  GEMM-driving prompt, max |delta logprob| 0.021-0.040, inside the F-05 band
  and below this fork's own g64 ternary figure of 0.061. Repack buffer 1759.50
  MiB = 252 of 254 ternary tensors. Dual residency covers the new type: 252
  shadows, graph splits tracking mode 0 rather than mode 1, bit-identical to
  mode 1 under --no-op-offload. The shared g64 kernels were rewritten here, so
  they were re-run against the pre-change answer key: 36 cells, every token
  identical, every logprob delta 0.000000000.
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5
Conflict-resolved: auto: affix(one side only added lines around the base, other side edited it) in ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch-fallback.h
